# The extraction gate, second batch: the cases that take extract_gate to 50 (34 cases).
#
# Only the new cases live here; the original 36 stay in `graph-build.jsonl` so the run of
# 2026-09-24 and its recording stay reproducible as published. `edge` is not extended here:
# it has three labels and an abstention band, and its one confident error was a literal
# reading of a ruling rather than a threshold problem, so more cases at the same shape would
# not move it. It gets its own treatment after the binary points have precision targets.
#
# The first batch scored 15/16 and its skips were mostly obvious furniture: navigation bars,
# cookie notices, tables of contents. Real crawls are not that kind. This batch keeps the
# same question and makes both sides harder, in four families:
#
#  A. Boilerplate that nonetheless carries an instance. A copyright footer naming a company,
#     a breadcrumb naming a ruling, a form label prefilled with a name, an email signature.
#     The question is whether an instance of the wanted kinds is present, not whether the
#     chunk is prose. These are `extract`.
#  B. Fluent prose with no instance at all. Legal doctrine, methodology, an editorial about
#     corporate structures in general. It reads like the target and contains nothing to pull
#     out. These are `skip`.
#  C. Wrong kind. A chunk dense with instances of a kind nobody asked for: dates and amounts
#     when the target is people and companies, place names when the target is rulings. `skip`.
#  D. One instance, buried. A long administrative preamble with a single name in the last
#     clause. `extract`, because one edge is why the gate exists.
#
# `looking_for` is varied beyond the two targets of the first batch, so that the point is not
# measured only on the two domains it was written against.
#
# RELABELLED after the first run, on the question's own criteria and not because the model
# disagreed: eg-41 was authored as `skip` because it looked like a table of attributes. The
# question asks whether the chunk "names at least one instance and says something about it",
# and a cadastral reference names one specific property and the chunk then states its
# surface, its year and its share. It is an `extract`. Published both ways in
# `docs/results/2026-09-24-fifty/`: 29/34 before the correction and 30/34 after.
#
# eg-47 was left alone deliberately, although the evaluator missed it at confidence 0.68. It
# is a breadcrumb naming a resolution number and no company or person, and the question's
# `false` side lists section paths explicitly. The label is arguable in both directions, and
# moving an arguable label to match the model is how a bench stops measuring anything.
#
# DISPUTED, recorded and not resolved: the blind second annotator also read eg-47 as `skip`,
# and read eg-25 and eg-35 as `skip` against the author's `extract`. All three are breadcrumbs
# or section headers that name an instance, which is family A above and the family this point
# disagrees with itself about: kappa between the two annotators is 0.82 here, the second
# lowest of the six points. That is a question-definition problem rather than a threshold one,
# and more cases of the same shape will not settle it. See
# `docs/results/2026-09-24-fifty/README.md` section 4.
#
# Annotator 1 (author) labelled every case before any decision was run; Annotator 2 is a
# separate generative model, blind to that label and to the evaluator's answer. Agreement is
# reported in the results directory, and the paper says where it uses these numbers that one
# annotator is a model and this is not two independent humans.
